## 5 Two particle correlations

### Introduction

Two particle correlations in pseudorapidity and azimuth is a way, how to trigger and point out effects bound to jet production in high energy particle collisions. The method is based on finding a high or mid \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) particle that acts as a trigger and correlating spatial distribution of less energetic particles within the event with respect to this trigger particle. Trigger high \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) particle is likely to be a result of a jet or hard parton scattering, thus it is likely, that the particle correlation will point out effects corresponding to jets in hot and dense medium.

### Method basics

When a trigger particle is selected, full azimuth and rapidity range (-1,1) is used to make use of the BEMC and TPC coverage. Trigger particle is selected solely due to its \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) or energy deposed in BEMC. The trigger particle properties, \(\eta\) and \(\Phi\) are noted. All is done for each of centrality bins in Table 5.1. Centrality is determined according to event multiplicity, which is defined as number of primary charged tracks detected by the TPC within \(|\eta|<0.5\).

Associated particles are selected from the same event as the trigger particle. Associated particles can be selected in multiple \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) bins if the statistics is sufficient, or a single \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) range can be defined. Always one must take associated particles with \(\mathrm{p_{T}}\) lower then the one of the trigger particle to avoid correlating trigger particle to itself. Properties of associated particle \(\eta\) and \(\Phi\) are recovered and \(\Delta\eta\) and \(\Delta\Phi\) of trigger and associated particle is stored into histogram. Possible values of \(\Delta\eta\) are (-2,2) and \(\Delta\Phi\) (-\(\pi\), \(\pi\)). All possible combinations of trigger-particle association are examined within an event. Therefore one particle can be associated to multiple trigger particles in case that event contains more then one particle. Resulting \(\Delta\eta\) x \(\Delta\Phi\) histogram has a form of a shape (Figure 5.1) long in \(\Delta\Phi\) and "A" shaped in \(\Delta\eta\), let's call it the roof, with jet-like effects superimposed on the roof. It is a good assumption that the jet-like effects should be, at least partially, visible already on the roof shape. Multiple corrections will take place to extract the effects, but those should not be of similar magnitude as the effect itself.

